e it and build something that users  actually want and it's been empirically  shown that large projects are much less  successful than a chain of smaller  projects that actually do the same thing  so now that I've convinced you to build  an MVP how do you scope that so this is  our process for doing that so first we  start with the requirements that came  out of that design sprint then we add in  non-functional requirements so these are  the things that engineering care is  about but don't describe things that  maybe users are doing in the product so  things like security making sure that  it's scalable reliability things like  that so once we have all of those  requirements in mind we write out a  feature story and user stories and you  can think of these stories like  it's for writing out your requirements  that force you to enumerate what the  goal is and what the business  constraints are so these are this gets  you towards more precise specifications  of what your requirements are next we  enumerate all the work that's required  to satisfy these stories and all of the  dependencies for that work and finally  you want to arrange the work to get  feedback as quickly as possible so this  is how our white board looks midway  through the process so the writing on  the board has our feature story and the  post-its are the units of work that meet  that are needed to satisfy those stories  and once we're done we can reorder those  post-its in such a way that we can learn  as quickly as possible  so these post-its describe our entire  plan but our MVP is just going to be a  subset of these throughout this process  users are going to tell you what what  they really want and your plans are  going to get disrupted so all those  post-its a lot of them where you may not  end up doing but there's nothing wrong  with that for example when we did our  design sprint our users didn't express  much interest in our in their notebooks  because they were much more used to  doing things with our studio and our  markdown but when our our users saw  Python notebooks in platform they wanted  it and we realized we needed to move our  kernel support and Jupiter up on the  roadmap so next I'm going to talk about  what we built and not just the MVP that  we built but the full architecture so  these are the components of our  architecture there's Jupiter there's a  custom built authentication proxy which  we call the front door there's  containerized notebooks running on  kubernetes and then there's routing  using an open source product called  traffic and next I'm going to take a  technical deep dive into each of these  so an idea that you're going to hear  over and over again is security so we  take security very seriously at civis  and all of our architectural decisions  are made with security in mind from the  start users work with sensitive client  data in our platform and in their  notebooks so security is important and  usable security was the focus of my PhD  and I'm Bea I was the engineering lead  on this project so I was interested in  making  sure that we can make things more secure  but also do this without constraining  users and hopefully you'll see how that  plays out in our architectural decisions  so the first component of our  architecture is Jupiter and we chose  Jupiter like Laurie said because it's  the clear winner in the notebook  ecosystem this means that users can  bring their existing notebooks into our  implementation easily and also take them  out easily and one thing that's  important is that we use Jupiter without  modification we didn't fork it and this  meant that we were able to upgrade to  the latest version of Jupiter the day it  was released so the next component of  our architecture is our authentication  proxy so this X is the front door to  your notebook and the front door ensures  that only authorized users can get into  the notebook you can access your  notebooks from anywhere as long as you  have internet access